---
import Layout from "@components/Layout.astro";
import PageHeader from "@components/PageHeader.astro";
import CardGrid from "@components/CardGrid.astro";
import DecklistTooltip from "@components/DecklistTooltip.astro";
import { Select, Badge, Card } from "webcoreui/astro";
import MatchupCard from "@components/MatchupCard.astro";
import WipAlert from "@components/WipAlert.astro";
import MatchupFilter from "@components/MatchupFilter.astro";
---

<Layout
    title="Matchups - Replenish Guide"
    description="Detailed matchup analysis for Replenish in Premodern. Learn how to beat The Rock, Sligh, Goblins, Burn, and other tier 1 strategies."
>
    <PageHeader
        title="Matchup Analysis"
        description="Detailed archetype evaluation for Premodern Replenish. Master strategic role assignment, essential sideboarding transitions, and high-leverage counterplay against tier 1 and competitive strategies."
        isCard={true}
    />

    <WipAlert />
    <div
        class="matchups-container"
        style="margin-top: 1.5rem; margin-bottom: 3rem;"
    >
        <MatchupFilter />

        <div
            class="matchups-list"
            style="display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 2rem;"
        >
            <MatchupCard
                matchup="enchantress"
                category="dominant"
                title="Enchantress"
                badgeTheme="success"
                badgeText="Dominant"
                subtitle="Faster combo assembly & superior graveyard refueling"
                isOpen={true}
            >
                <CardGrid
                    cards={[
                        {
                            src: "/src/assets/cards/matchups/enchantress/argothian_enchantress.jpg",
                            alt: "Argothian Enchantress",
                        },
                        {
                            src: "/src/assets/cards/matchups/enchantress/enchantresss_presence.jpg",
                            alt: "Enchantress's Presence",
                        },
                        {
                            src: "/src/assets/cards/matchups/enchantress/sterling_grove.jpg",
                            alt: "Sterling Grove",
                        },
                    ]}
                />
                <p>
                    <strong>Core Gameplan:</strong> Historically our most dominant
                    matchup. Replenish consistently assembles an operational <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Opalescence">Opalescence</a
                    > and <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Parallax Wave">Parallax Wave</a
                    > lock faster and with greater logistical redundancy than traditional
                    Enchantress configurations can race.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <strong>Navigating the Lock:</strong> The primary post-sideboard
                    obstacle involves bypassing their protective layers (typically
                    dual instances of <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Sterling Grove">Sterling Grove</a
                    >) and the debilitating tax of <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Aura of Silence">Aura of Silence</a
                    >. Victory demands clinical patience: retain at least one
                    instant-speed <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Seal of Cleansing">Seal of Cleansing</a
                    > or <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Disenchant">Disenchant</a
                    > strictly to neutralize an inbound <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Decree of Silence">Decree of Silence</a
                    > before committing to terminal Replenish resolutions.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <strong>Sideboard Strategy:</strong> Transition out unnecessary
                    creature interaction (<a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Swords to Plowshares"
                        >Swords to Plowshares</a
                    >) and anti-land tech (<a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Tsabo's Web">Tsabo's Web</a
                    >). Bring in additional copies of <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Seal of Cleansing">Seal of Cleansing</a
                    >, <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Abeyance"
                        >Abeyance</a
                    >, and <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Arcane Denial">Arcane Denial</a
                    >. Because Enchantress frequently overloads on passive
                    graveyard and enchantment disruption after Game 1,
                    introducing <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Exalted Angel">Exalted Angel</a
                    > provides an exceptional alternate axis—effortlessly swinging
                    through their passive hate while their hand clogs with dead reactive
                    cards.
                </p>
            </MatchupCard>
            <MatchupCard
                matchup="elves"
                category="dominant"
                title="Elves"
                badgeTheme="success"
                badgeText="Dominant"
                subtitle="Overwhelming endgame combo lock & high-leverage removal"
            >
                <CardGrid
                    cards={[
                        {
                            src: "/src/assets/cards/matchups/elves/priest_of_titania.jpg",
                            alt: "Priest of Titania",
                        },
                        {
                            src: "/src/assets/cards/matchups/elves/quirion_ranger.jpg",
                            alt: "Quirion Ranger",
                        },
                        {
                            src: "/src/assets/cards/matchups/elves/wirewood_symbiote.jpg",
                            alt: "Wirewood Symbiote",
                        },
                    ]}
                />
                <p>
                    <strong>Core Gameplan:</strong> Elves represents a highly favorable
                    structural matchup. Once an <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Opalescence">Opalescence</a
                    > plus <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Parallax Wave">Parallax Wave</a
                    > lock hits the battlefield, their entire board is permanently
                    exiled. Utilize early copies of <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Swords to Plowshares"
                        >Swords to Plowshares</a
                    > exclusively to sever foundational mana acceleration (<a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Priest of Titania">Priest of Titania</a
                    > or <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Rofellos, Llanowar Emissary">Rofellos</a
                    >). While Elves possesses explosive combo potential,
                    stabilizing into Turn 4 guarantees an insurmountable
                    resource lock.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <strong>Sideboard Strategy:</strong> Remove <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Tsabo's Web">Tsabo's Web</a
                    > unless opposing lists display non-basic mana disruption like
                    <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Rishadan Port">Rishadan Port</a
                    > or <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Wasteland">Wasteland</a
                    >. Trimming <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Meddling Mage">Meddling Mage</a
                    > is acceptable to maximize reactive efficiency. Bring in additional
                    spot removal (<a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Swords to Plowshares"
                        >Swords to Plowshares</a
                    >), defensive sweepers (<a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Wrath of God">Wrath of God</a
                    >), and <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Seal of Cleansing">Seal of Cleansing</a
                    > to combat post-board zero-mana interactions like <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Reverent Silence">Reverent Silence</a
                    >.
                </p>
            </MatchupCard>
            <MatchupCard
                matchup="rock_and_midrange"
                category="dominant"
                title="Rock and Midrange"
                badgeTheme="success"
                badgeText="Dominant"
                subtitle="Massive card advantage engine overcomes one-for-one attrition"
            >
                <CardGrid
                    cards={[
                        {
                            src: "/src/assets/cards/matchups/rock_and_midrange/pernicious_deed.jpg",
                            alt: "Pernicious Deed",
                        },
                        {
                            src: "/src/assets/cards/matchups/rock_and_midrange/spiritmonger.jpg",
                            alt: "Spiritmonger",
                        },
                        {
                            src: "/src/assets/cards/matchups/rock_and_midrange/cabal_therapy.jpg",
                            alt: "Cabal Therapy",
                        },
                    ]}
                />
                <p>
                    <strong>Core Gameplan:</strong> Traditional black-green midrange
                    architectures rely heavily on one-for-one attrition (<a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Cabal Therapy">Cabal Therapy</a
                    >, <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Duress"
                        >Duress</a
                    >, <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Pernicious Deed">Pernicious Deed</a
                    >). This strategic profile fails to keep pace with the
                    massive card advantage and graveyard refueling engine of <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Replenish">Replenish</a
                    >. Lacking a rapid, disruptive early clock, they grant ample
                    time to assemble complete lock redundancy.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <strong>Sideboard Strategy:</strong> In post-board games, anticipate
                    high-impact graveyard interaction such as <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Withered Wretch">Withered Wretch</a
                    > and color taxing via <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Gloom">Gloom</a
                    >. Trim card-disadvantage filtration like <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Frantic Search">Frantic Search</a
                    > in favor of proactive threats (<a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Exalted Angel">Exalted Angel</a
                    >) and direct permanent removal (<a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Seal of Cleansing">Seal of Cleansing</a
                    > and <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Disenchant">Disenchant</a
                    >). Preserve copies of <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Swords to Plowshares"
                        >Swords to Plowshares</a
                    > strictly to terminate early Wretches before committing combo
                    pieces to the graveyard.
                </p>
            </MatchupCard>
            <MatchupCard
                matchup="sligh"
                category="favorable"
                title="Sligh"
                badgeTheme="info"
                badgeText="Favorable"
                subtitle="Superior survival architecture overcomes burn clocks & lock finishes"
            >
                <CardGrid
                    cards={[
                        {
                            src: "/src/assets/cards/matchups/sligh/jackal_pup.jpg",
                            alt: "Jackal Pup",
                        },
                        {
                            src: "/src/assets/cards/matchups/sligh/fireblast.jpg",
                            alt: "Fireblast",
                        },
                        {
                            src: "/src/assets/cards/matchups/sligh/ball_lightning.jpg",
                            alt: "Ball Lightning",
                        },
                    ]}
                />
                <p>
                    <strong>Core Gameplan:</strong> Sligh possesses nearly zero meaningful
                    stack or permanent interaction against our combo architecture;
                    their sole strategic victory vector is pacing lethal burn before
                    we stabilize. When deploying early copies of <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Meddling Mage">Meddling Mage</a
                    >, defensively name <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Lightning Bolt">Lightning Bolt</a
                    > to preserve Mage's 2/2 body as an unassailable blocker against
                    <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Jackal Pup"
                        >Jackal Pup</a
                    > and Goblin drop-ins. As the game develops into the mid-to-late
                    turns, shift subsequent Mage naming strictly to terminal reach
                    spells like <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Fireblast">Fireblast</a
                    > or <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Ball Lightning">Ball Lightning</a
                    >.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <strong>Sideboard Strategy:</strong> Immediately excise all copies
                    of <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Ancient Tomb">Ancient Tomb</a
                    >; the two-life surcharge per activation presents an
                    unacceptably suicidal liability against red burn. Bring in <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Exalted Angel">Exalted Angel</a
                    >, <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Hydroblast">Hydroblast</a
                    >, and your fourth <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Meddling Mage">Meddling Mage</a
                    >. In post-board games, treat every defensive spell as a
                    targeted resource exchange designed simply to absorb one
                    opposing burn card—your overarching posture is bridging
                    safely to the endgame combo, not entering an open damage
                    race.<br />
                    <em>Defensive Hate Suite Evaluation:</em>
                    <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Chill"
                        >Chill</a
                    > functions as our definitive MVP because it systematically shuts
                    down their ability to clinch victories via topdecked basic Mountains
                    into cheap direct damage after you stabilize at low life thresholds.
                    <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Sphere of Law">Sphere of Law</a
                    > provides immense structural protection but remains vulnerable
                    to splash naturalize effects and feels sluggish on the draw. Strictly
                    disregard <strong>Circle of Protection: Red</strong>;
                    continuously taxing your own generic mana development
                    directly undermines your capacity to execute critical setup
                    and combo lines.
                </p>
            </MatchupCard>
            <MatchupCard
                matchup="landstill"
                category="favorable"
                title="Landstill"
                badgeTheme="info"
                badgeText="Favorable"
                subtitle="Overwhelming threat density exhausts reactive countermagic"
            >
                <CardGrid
                    cards={[
                        {
                            src: "/src/assets/cards/matchups/landstill/standstill.jpg",
                            alt: "Standstill",
                        },
                        {
                            src: "/src/assets/cards/matchups/landstill/mishras_factory.jpg",
                            alt: "Mishra's Factory",
                        },
                        {
                            src: "/src/assets/cards/matchups/landstill/counterspell.jpg",
                            alt: "Counterspell",
                        },
                    ]}
                />
                <p>
                    <strong>Core Gameplan:</strong> Landstill relies on slow, incremental
                    advantage and typically fields a restricted density of active
                    blue mana sources on the board. Your overarching objective is
                    to accumulate resources patiently and then overload their blue
                    mana entirely within a single critical turn cycle. While they
                    can often counter one or two test spells, initiating your sequence
                    when they have only 3 to 4 open blue sources consistently ensures
                    your lethal <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Replenish">Replenish</a
                    > resolves as the terminal spell on the stack.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <strong
                        >Tactical Tools (Chant & Abeyance vs. Mana Short):</strong
                    > In post-board games, strictly prioritize low-cost interaction
                    like <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Orim's Chant">Orim's Chant</a
                    > and <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Abeyance">Abeyance</a
                    > over costly alternatives like <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Mana Short">Mana Short</a
                    >. Because Chant and Abeyance cost just one and two mana
                    respectively, you can deploy them as high-leverage
                    "must-counter" threats without taxing your own mana
                    development. Most importantly, resolving an Abeyance or
                    Chant completely shuts off zero-mana interaction like <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Foil">Foil</a
                    > or <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Thwart"
                        >Thwart</a
                    >—which Mana Short entirely fails to intercept—while
                    Abeyance provides the dual utility of drawing a card and
                    locking out <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Mishra's Factory">Mishra's Factory</a
                    > and cycling activations.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <strong>Deploying Meddling Mage:</strong> When deploying an early
                    <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Meddling Mage">Meddling Mage</a
                    > on turn 2, evaluate your immediate follow-up lines for turns
                    3 and 4 before casting it. If you are preparing a decisive combo
                    turn and simply want to consume their countermagic or mana, name
                    <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Counterspell"
                        >Counterspell</a
                    >. If your hand requires proactive board presence to apply
                    combat pressure while retaining the body, name <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Swords to Plowshares"
                        >Swords to Plowshares</a
                    > or their primary card draw engine.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <strong>Sideboard Execution:</strong> Proactively deploy early
                    permanent solutions—specifically bringing in your 4th <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Seal of Cleansing">Seal of Cleansing</a
                    > and copies of <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Tsabo's Web">Tsabo's Web</a
                    >. An active Seal sitting on the battlefield completely
                    neutralizes their ability to stick a <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Standstill">Standstill</a
                    >, while Tsabo's Web dismantles both their operational mana
                    production and their Mishra's Factory combat clock. <em
                        >Note:</em
                    > Strictly avoid utilizing <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Deep Analysis">Deep Analysis</a
                    > against Landstill and UW Dreadnought variants running Fling,
                    as incurring a voluntary 3-life liability severely compromises
                    your survival against high-impact finish lines.
                </p>
            </MatchupCard>
            <MatchupCard
                matchup="psychatog"
                category="favorable"
                title="Psychatog"
                badgeTheme="info"
                badgeText="Favorable"
                subtitle="High-leverage threat overload beats slow developmental control"
            >
                <CardGrid
                    cards={[
                        {
                            src: "/src/assets/cards/matchups/psychatog/psychatog.jpg",
                            alt: "Psychatog",
                        },
                        {
                            src: "/src/assets/cards/matchups/psychatog/upheaval.jpg",
                            alt: "Upheaval",
                        },
                        {
                            src: "/src/assets/cards/matchups/psychatog/circular_logic.jpg",
                            alt: "Circular Logic",
                        },
                    ]}
                />
                <p>
                    <strong>Core Gameplan:</strong> Sharing tactical parallels with
                    Landstill, Psychatog attempts to control early development while
                    building toward a terminal <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Upheaval">Upheaval</a
                    > plus <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Psychatog">Psychatog</a
                    > finish. However, Replenish boasts an overwhelming density of
                    high-impact threats (<a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Opalescence">Opalescence</a
                    >, <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Parallax Wave">Parallax Wave</a
                    >, <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Replenish"
                        >Replenish</a
                    >) that consistently exhausts their countermagic and removal
                    capabilities before reaching their mana threshold.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <strong>Sideboard Strategy:</strong> Execute a full control transformation.
                    Introduce defensive taxers like <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Defense Grid">Defense Grid</a
                    > and <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Meddling Mage">Meddling Mage</a
                    > alongside high-leverage instant disruption (<a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Abeyance">Abeyance</a
                    >, <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Arcane Denial">Arcane Denial</a
                    >). Because Psychatog sideboards heavily into passive
                    anti-enchantment suites and card draw disruption,
                    transitioning into <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Exalted Angel">Exalted Angel</a
                    > provides a dominant clock that completely sidesteps their counter-measures.
                </p>
            </MatchupCard>
            <MatchupCard
                matchup="bw_control"
                category="favorable"
                title="BW Control"
                badgeTheme="info"
                badgeText="Favorable"
                subtitle="Opposing discard actively accelerates our Replenish graveyard setup"
            >
                <CardGrid
                    cards={[
                        {
                            src: "/src/assets/cards/matchups/bw_control/vindicate.jpg",
                            alt: "Vindicate",
                        },
                        {
                            src: "/src/assets/cards/matchups/bw_control/gerrards_verdict.jpg",
                            alt: "Gerrard's Verdict",
                        },
                        {
                            src: "/src/assets/cards/matchups/bw_control/swords_to_plowshares.jpg",
                            alt: "Swords to Plowshares",
                        },
                    ]}
                />
                <p>
                    <strong>Core Gameplan:</strong> Orzhov control variants leverage
                    oppressive hand disruption (<a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Gerrard's Verdict">Gerrard's Verdict</a
                    >, <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Duress"
                        >Duress</a
                    >) paired with versatile permanent destruction (<a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Vindicate">Vindicate</a
                    >, <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Swords to Plowshares"
                        >Swords to Plowshares</a
                    >). In practice, this discard strategy actively plays into
                    our strengths: discarding fatal enchantment clusters simply
                    accelerates our graveyard setup for a terminal <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Replenish">Replenish</a
                    > resolution.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <strong>Sideboard Strategy:</strong> Anticipate heavy graveyard
                    disruption (<a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Withered Wretch">Withered Wretch</a
                    >, <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Tormod's Crypt">Tormod's Crypt</a
                    >) and enchantment tax structures (<a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Aura of Silence">Aura of Silence</a
                    >). Bring in <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Exalted Angel">Exalted Angel</a
                    > as an unstoppable morph threat, alongside instant-speed answers
                    like <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Disenchant">Disenchant</a
                    > and <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Seal of Cleansing">Seal of Cleansing</a
                    >. Avoid prolonged resource standoffs; force decisive
                    exchanges while their removal targets are misaligned.
                </p>
            </MatchupCard>
            <MatchupCard
                matchup="rg_ponza"
                category="favorable"
                title="RG Ponza"
                badgeTheme="info"
                badgeText="Favorable"
                subtitle="Basic mana discipline & post-board permanent locks defeat land destruction"
            >
                <CardGrid
                    cards={[
                        {
                            src: "/src/assets/cards/matchups/rg_ponza/stone_rain.jpg",
                            alt: "Stone Rain",
                        },
                        {
                            src: "/src/assets/cards/matchups/rg_ponza/pillage.jpg",
                            alt: "Pillage",
                        },
                        {
                            src: "/src/assets/cards/matchups/rg_ponza/rishadan_port.jpg",
                            alt: "Rishadan Port",
                        },
                    ]}
                />
                <p>
                    <strong>Core Gameplan:</strong> This matchup is heavily play/draw
                    dependent. Your primary objective is protecting basic mana production
                    to consistently satisfy land drops through continuous land destruction
                    (<a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Stone Rain"
                        >Stone Rain</a
                    >, <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Pillage"
                        >Pillage</a
                    >). Aggressively mulligan vulnerable two-land hands if they
                    lack basic mana development or early logistical filtration
                    to insulate against early disruption.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <strong>Sideboard Strategy:</strong> Transition out fragile early
                    creature interaction like <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Meddling Mage">Meddling Mage</a
                    > in favor of high-impact finishers (<a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Exalted Angel">Exalted Angel</a
                    >). Deploying <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Tsabo's Web">Tsabo's Web</a
                    > is strictly essential to dismantle their reliance on <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Rishadan Port">Rishadan Port</a
                    > and <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Wasteland">Wasteland</a
                    >—even in configurations running our own factories, Web's
                    asymmetric taxing advantage far outweighs any internal
                    synergy sacrifice.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <strong>Tactical Considerations:</strong> Preserve at least 2–3
                    copies of <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Swords to Plowshares"
